---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ColumnToListTool from '../../components/ColumnToListTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { columnToList, PRESETS } from '../../lib/list-convert';

const tool = toolBySlug('column-to-comma-separated-list');

// Every example below is generated by the real engine, so docs and behaviour can't drift.
const sample = 'apple\nbanana\ncherry';
const examples = PRESETS.map((p) => ({ ...p, output: columnToList(sample, p.options).output }));
const idSample = '1042\n1043\n1044';
const sqlExample = columnToList(idSample, { delimiter: ', ' }).output;
const sqlQuoteGotcha = columnToList("O'Brien\nSmith", PRESETS.find((p) => p.id === 'sql')!.options).output;
const versionNumeric = columnToList('v2.10\nv2.9\nv2.1', { sort: 'num-asc' }).output;
const versionNatural = columnToList('v2.10\nv2.9\nv2.1', { sort: 'az' }).output;
const trailingNewline = columnToList('a\nb\n');

const steps = [
  '<strong>Paste your column.</strong> It can come from Excel, Google Sheets, a SQL result, a log file or any text editor; each line is one item.',
  '<strong>Pick an output shape.</strong> Click a preset chip (SQL IN, JSON array, Python list…) or set the separator and wrappers by hand in the options panel.',
  '<strong>Tidy the list if you need to.</strong> Remove duplicates, sort A–Z or numerically, change the letter case, or reverse the order.',
  '<strong>Copy or download.</strong> The result updates as you type; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> in the input box copies without reaching for the mouse.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is my data uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The conversion runs entirely in your browser with JavaScript. Nothing you paste is sent to a server, stored, or logged. You can disconnect from the internet after the page loads and it will keep working.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I put quotes around each value?',
    a: 'Type a quote character into both the <em>Before</em> and <em>After</em> boxes under “Wrap each item”, or click the <strong>SQL IN</strong>, <strong>JSON array</strong> or <strong>Python list</strong> preset, which fills those boxes for you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use a separator other than a comma?',
    a: 'Yes. The separator menu includes semicolon, pipe, space, tab and new line. Pick <em>Custom…</em> to type anything else, such as <code>--</code> or <code> :: </code>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the item count say some lines were dropped?',
    a: 'Blank lines are skipped by default, and duplicates are removed when that option is ticked. The counter tells you how many lines did not make it into the output so nothing disappears silently. Untick “Skip blank lines” to keep them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work with data copied from Excel or Google Sheets?',
    a: 'Yes. Select a column in your spreadsheet, copy it, and paste here. Each cell becomes one line, which is exactly what the converter expects. Trailing spaces from cells are trimmed automatically.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I go the other way — from a comma list back into a column?',
    a: 'Use the companion <a href="/tools/comma-separated-list-to-column">comma separated list to column</a> tool, or click <em>Split back to column</em> under the result box, which opens that tool with your list already loaded.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <ColumnToListTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Example: what each preset produces</h2>
    <p>
      Starting from the three-line column <code>apple</code>, <code>banana</code>, <code>cherry</code>, each preset gives you a
      different, ready-to-paste result:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Preset</th><th>Output</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {examples.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{e.label}</strong></td>
              <td><code>{e.output}</code></td>
              <td>{
                {
                  plain: 'Sentences, emails, tags fields',
                  compact: 'CSV cells, URL query strings',
                  sql: 'WHERE id IN (…) clauses',
                  json: 'API payloads, config files',
                  python: 'Scripts and notebooks',
                  semicolon: 'Outlook / Exchange recipient lists',
                  pipe: 'Markdown tables, log filters',
                  regex: 'Alternation patterns in search tools',
                }[e.id]
              }</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Common situations where this saves time</h2>
    <h3>Building a SQL <code>IN</code> clause</h3>
    <p>
      You have a column of IDs from a report and need to query those exact rows. Paste the IDs, click <strong>SQL IN</strong>, and
      drop the result straight after <code>WHERE id IN</code>. For numeric IDs you may prefer no quotes at all — use the <strong>Plain</strong>
      preset and wrap the whole list in parentheses:
    </p>
    <pre><code>SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id IN ({sqlExample});</code></pre>

    <h3>Addressing an email to a list of people</h3>
    <p>
      Most mail clients accept addresses separated by commas; Outlook traditionally expects semicolons. Paste the address column,
      choose the matching preset, and paste the result into the To or BCC field. Tick <em>Remove duplicates</em> first so nobody
      receives the message twice.
    </p>

    <h3>Turning spreadsheet rows into code</h3>
    <p>
      Product SKUs, country codes, feature flags — data often starts life in a spreadsheet and needs to end up in an array. The <strong>JSON array</strong> and <strong>Python list</strong> presets add the right quotes and brackets so the result parses
      on the first try; paste the result into the <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a> if you want it validated.
    </p>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Presets</strong> (No spaces, Plain, SQL IN, JSON array, Python list, Semicolon, Pipe, Regex OR) fill the separator and wrapper boxes in one click. <strong>No spaces</strong> is what the page starts on. The highlighted chip is the preset that matches the current settings; edit any box by hand and the highlight clears.</li>
      <li><strong>Between items</strong> is the separator: Comma only (the default), Comma + space, Semicolon + space, Semicolon only, Pipe with spaces, Pipe only, Space, Tab, New line, or <em>Custom…</em> for anything you type, including several characters such as <code> :: </code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Wrap each item — Before / After</strong> adds text around every item, e.g. <code>'</code> and <code>'</code> for SQL string literals. Nothing inside the item is escaped (see edge cases).</li>
      <li><strong>Wrap whole list — Start / End</strong> adds text once at the beginning and end, e.g. <code>(</code> and <code>)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim spaces around items</strong> (on by default) removes leading and trailing spaces and tabs from each line before anything else happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Skip blank lines</strong> (on by default) drops lines that are empty after trimming and reports them as dropped. Untick it to keep them as empty items: <code>a, , b</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove duplicates</strong> keeps the first occurrence of each item; <strong>…ignoring letter case</strong> makes <code>Apple</code>, <code>apple</code> and <code>APPLE</code> collapse to whichever was written first.</li>
      <li><strong>Reverse order</strong> is applied last, after sorting, so A → Z plus Reverse gives the same result as Z → A.</li>
      <li><strong>Sort</strong>: <em>A → Z</em> and <em>Z → A</em> use natural order (<code>item2</code> before <code>item10</code>, case-insensitive); <em>Numeric, low → high</em> and <em>high → low</em> parse each item as a number; <em>Shortest first</em> and <em>Longest first</em> order by length and break ties alphabetically.</li>
      <li><strong>Letter case</strong>: Keep as is, lowercase, UPPERCASE, or Title Case. Case is applied before duplicates are removed, so lowercase plus Remove duplicates merges <code>Apple</code> and <code>apple</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Copy to clipboard</strong>, <strong>Download .txt</strong>, and <strong>Split back to column →</strong>, which opens the <a href="/tools/comma-separated-list-to-column">reverse tool</a> with this result already loaded.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Quotes inside values are not escaped.</strong> <code>O'Brien</code> with the SQL IN preset produces <code>{sqlQuoteGotcha}</code>, which is not valid SQL, and a value containing <code>"</code> breaks the JSON array preset the same way. Fix those values by hand, or run the JSON result through the <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a>, which will show exactly where it breaks.</li>
      <li><strong>Values containing the separator</strong> — a line such as <code>Smith, John</code> becomes two items to anything that reads the output as CSV. Choose a separator that does not occur in the data (Pipe or Tab), or wrap each item in quotes.</li>
      <li><strong>Numeric sort reads the first number it can find</strong> after stripping everything except digits, <code>.</code> and <code>-</code>: <code>$42</code> sorts as 42, <code>1,234.5</code> as 1234.5, and lines with no digits go last. Version strings do not work this way — <code>v2.10</code> reads as 2.1 and sorts as <code>{versionNumeric}</code>. Use <em>A → Z</em> instead, which compares digit runs naturally: <code>{versionNatural}</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>A trailing newline counts as a blank line.</strong> Three lines followed by Enter shows “{trailingNewline.count} items · {trailingNewline.dropped} dropped”; nothing is missing, the final empty line was skipped.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows line endings</strong> (<code>\r\n</code>) and old-Mac <code>\r</code> are recognised, so pasting from any editor works.</li>
      <li><strong>Tabs inside a line are kept.</strong> Two spreadsheet columns copied together arrive as <code>a[tab]b</code> per line and become one item each. Copy a single column at a time.</li>
      <li><strong>Title Case here is the simple first-letter rule</strong>: <code>o'neil mcdonald</code> becomes <code>O'neil Mcdonald</code> and <code>USA</code> becomes <code>Usa</code>. For style-guide capitalisation, run the result through the <a href="/tools/title-case-converter">Title Case Converter</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Input made only of blank lines</strong> produces an empty result and the counter reports every line as dropped; a completely empty box shows “0 lines” and nothing else.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same job in a spreadsheet or in code</h2>
    <h3>Excel and Google Sheets</h3>
    <p>The <code>TEXTJOIN</code> function joins a range with a separator and can skip blank cells:</p>
    <pre><code>=TEXTJOIN(", ", TRUE, A1:A100)</code></pre>
    <p>To add quotes around every value, wrap the range in a text expression first:</p>
    <pre><code>=TEXTJOIN(", ", TRUE, "'" &amp; A1:A100 &amp; "'")</code></pre>

    <h3>Python</h3>
    <pre><code>lines = [l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()]
result = ", ".join(lines)</code></pre>

    <h3>Command line</h3>
    <pre><code>paste -sd, column.txt          # Linux / macOS
(Get-Content column.txt) -join ", "   # PowerShell</code></pre>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
